SCENARIO 4: FIX 1: SELECT ... FOR UPDATE (pessimistic locking)
Each session locks the row when it reads it. B must wait until A commits,
then reads A's new value.
==============================================================================
[  0.00s] A> BEGIN
           A  -> BEGIN
[  0.00s] A> SELECT recovered_bbls FROM accident WHERE report_number = 99990001 FOR UPDATE
           A  -> 300.00
[  0.00s] B> BEGIN
           B  -> BEGIN
[  0.00s] B> SELECT recovered_bbls FROM accident WHERE report_number = 99990001 FOR UPDATE
           (B is blocked, waiting for A's lock)
[  2.00s] A> UPDATE accident SET recovered_bbls = 350.00 WHERE report_number = 99990001
           A  -> UPDATE 1
[  2.00s] A> COMMIT
           A  -> COMMIT
           B  -> 350.00
[  2.00s] B> UPDATE accident SET recovered_bbls = 380.00 WHERE report_number = 99990001
           B  -> UPDATE 1
[  2.00s] B> COMMIT
           B  -> COMMIT
B waited 2.00 s for the lock
RESULT: recovered_bbls for 99990001 = 380.00  (CORRECT - both updates kept)

==============================================================================
SCENARIO 5: FIX 2: REPEATABLE READ detects the conflict (optimistic approach)
No locks are taken when reading. PostgreSQL refuses B's write because the
row changed after B's snapshot. The application retries B's transaction.
==============================================================================
[  0.00s] A> BEGIN ISOLATION LEVEL REPEATABLE READ
           A  -> BEGIN
[  0.00s] B> BEGIN ISOLATION LEVEL REPEATABLE READ
           B  -> BEGIN
[  0.00s] A> SELECT recovered_bbls FROM accident WHERE report_number = 99990001
           A  -> 300.00
[  0.00s] B> SELECT recovered_bbls FROM accident WHERE report_number = 99990001
           B  -> 300.00
[  0.00s] A> UPDATE accident SET recovered_bbls = 350.00 WHERE report_number = 99990001
           A  -> UPDATE 1
[  0.00s] A> COMMIT
           A  -> COMMIT
[  0.00s] B> UPDATE accident SET recovered_bbls = 330.00 WHERE report_number = 99990001
           B  -> ERROR:  could not serialize access due to concurrent update
[  0.00s] B> ROLLBACK
           B  -> ROLLBACK
           (application catches the serialization error and retries B)
[  0.00s] B> BEGIN ISOLATION LEVEL REPEATABLE READ
           B  -> BEGIN
[  0.00s] B> SELECT recovered_bbls FROM accident WHERE report_number = 99990001
           B  -> 350.00
[  0.00s] B> UPDATE accident SET recovered_bbls = 380.00 WHERE report_number = 99990001
           B  -> UPDATE 1
[  0.00s] B> COMMIT
           B  -> COMMIT
RESULT: recovered_bbls for 99990001 = 380.00  (CORRECT - conflict detected, retry applied)

