SCENARIO 7: Locking + CHECK constraint stop an impossible total
500 bbl were released and 300 recovered. A records +150 and B records +100,
which together would exceed what was released. With locking, B sees A's
value and the CHECK (recovered <= released) rejects B's update.
==============================================================================
[  0.00s] A> BEGIN
           A  -> BEGIN
[  0.00s] A> SELECT recovered_bbls FROM accident WHERE report_number = 99990001 FOR UPDATE
           A  -> 300.00
[  0.00s] B> BEGIN
           B  -> BEGIN
[  0.00s] B> SELECT recovered_bbls FROM accident WHERE report_number = 99990001 FOR UPDATE
[  1.50s] A> UPDATE accident SET recovered_bbls = 450.00 WHERE report_number = 99990001
           A  -> UPDATE 1
[  1.50s] A> COMMIT
           A  -> COMMIT
           B  -> 450.00
[  1.50s] B> UPDATE accident SET recovered_bbls = 550.00 WHERE report_number = 99990001
           B  -> ERROR:  new row for relation "accident" violates check constraint "chk_recovered_not_more_than_released"
[  1.50s] B> ROLLBACK
           B  -> ROLLBACK
RESULT: recovered_bbls for 99990001 = 450.00  (consistent - B's impossible update was refused and must be checked by B)
Without locking, B would have written 300 + 100 = 400, silently losing A's 150 bbl
and passing the CHECK. The constraint is only reliable when updates are serialised.

==============================================================================
SCENARIO 8: DEADLOCK (the problem)
A updates report 99990001 then 99990002. B updates the same two reports in the
OPPOSITE order. Each ends up waiting for a row the other has locked.
==============================================================================
[  0.00s] A> BEGIN
           A  -> BEGIN
[  0.00s] B> BEGIN
           B  -> BEGIN
[  0.00s] A> UPDATE accident SET recovered_bbls = recovered_bbls + 1 WHERE report_number = 99990001
           A  -> UPDATE 1
[  0.00s] B> UPDATE accident SET recovered_bbls = recovered_bbls + 1 WHERE report_number = 99990002
           B  -> UPDATE 1
[  0.00s] A> UPDATE accident SET recovered_bbls = recovered_bbls + 1 WHERE report_number = 99990002
[  0.30s] B> UPDATE accident SET recovered_bbls = recovered_bbls + 1 WHERE report_number = 99990001
           B  -> UPDATE 1
           A  -> ERROR:  deadlock detected
[  1.00s] A> ROLLBACK
           A  -> ROLLBACK
[  1.00s] B> COMMIT
           B  -> COMMIT
RESULT: PostgreSQL detected the deadlock after deadlock_timeout (1 s) and cancelled session A so the other could finish.

==============================================================================
SCENARIO 9: FIX: lock rows in a consistent order
Both sessions lock BOTH reports first, always in report_number order.
B simply waits for A instead of deadlocking.
==============================================================================
[  0.00s] A> BEGIN
           A  -> BEGIN
[  0.00s] A> SELECT report_number FROM accident WHERE report_number IN (99990001, 99990002) ORDER BY report_number FOR UPDATE
           A  -> [(99990001,), (99990002,)]
[  0.00s] B> BEGIN
           B  -> BEGIN
[  0.00s] B> SELECT report_number FROM accident WHERE report_number IN (99990001, 99990002) ORDER BY report_number FOR UPDATE
[  1.50s] A> UPDATE accident SET recovered_bbls = recovered_bbls + 1 WHERE report_number = 99990001
           A  -> UPDATE 1
[  1.50s] A> UPDATE accident SET recovered_bbls = recovered_bbls + 1 WHERE report_number = 99990002
           A  -> UPDATE 1
[  1.50s] A> COMMIT
           A  -> COMMIT
           B  -> [(99990001,), (99990002,)]
[  1.50s] B> UPDATE accident SET recovered_bbls = recovered_bbls + 1 WHERE report_number = 99990002
           B  -> UPDATE 1
[  1.50s] B> UPDATE accident SET recovered_bbls = recovered_bbls + 1 WHERE report_number = 99990001
           B  -> UPDATE 1
[  1.50s] B> COMMIT
           B  -> COMMIT
RESULT: 99990001 = 302.00, 99990002 = 302.00 - no deadlock, both sessions' changes applied (300 + 1 + 1)
